#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110419 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/id #uuid "aedb6e30-d1e6-5d89-aa83-56c1d6946b50" :question/math-academy-id "e-2904" :question/problem "Are the polygons $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ below congruent?\n\n![](images/75/75684fc94e43e8854403d543019f28f971b8b6adc0ca3f40cb673dc9dec6dcb9.png)" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\n$\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ have the same shape: They have congruent corresponding sides and congruent corresponding angles. This implies that $\\mathcal{P} \\cong \\mathcal{Q}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110791 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30fc8daf-a6eb-537b-b19b-06ad5504b5e6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons Given Side and Angle Measures"}] :question/id #uuid "fd18dfda-f385-5e24-a200-882a71d3b063" :question/math-academy-id "e-2908" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true for the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$, $\\mathcal{B}$, and $\\mathcal{C}$ shown below?\n\n1. $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$\n2. $\\mathcal{B}\\cong\\mathcal{C}$\n3. $\\mathcal{C}\\cong\\mathcal{A}$\n\n![](images/a1/a13f283489521fc43d73a5a128b79ff937dca760c8cea330706efe6f39ac26f6.png)" :question/worked-solution "In general, congruent polygons have the same shape. In other words:\n\n- they have the same number of sides,\n- all corresponding sides are congruent, and\n- all corresponding interior angles are congruent.\n\nWith that in mind, let's compare the given polygons.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since the polygons $\\mathcal{A}$ and $\\mathcal{B}$ have the same number of sides and all corresponding sides and angles are congruent, we conclude that $\\mathcal{A}\\cong\\mathcal{B}$.\n- Statement II is false. Notice that the polygons $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ have the same number of sides, but they don't seem to have the same shape. In particular, we notice they don't have corresponding congruent angles ($\\mathcal{B}$ does not have right angles, while $\\mathcal{C}$ does).\n- Statement III is false. By the same reasoning as above, we can see that the polygons $\\mathcal{C}$ and $\\mathcal{A}$ are not congruent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110792 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46dbecc9-70e8-55c9-b7d7-7b5629ea1549" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Congruence Statement"}] :question/id #uuid "b3ec8468-a64a-5f55-b392-8f0072fbdd78" :question/math-academy-id "e-2910" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9be6ab7fe0608003c7a0493b8aab19df0dd4353b085330fc2a8f8aea84e527.png)\n\nWrite down a correct congruence statement for the quadrilaterals shown in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $\\angle A \\cong \\angle C$.\n\nLet's determine the vertices in the second quadrilateral that correspond to $A$. First, notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= XY \\\\\nAD &= XW \\\\\n&= ∠A \\mid ≅∠X\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $X$, and\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $Z$ (since $C$ is opposite $A$ and $Z$ is opposite $X$).\n\nAs for the other two vertices, notice we have two more pairs of congruent angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}∠B & ≅∠Y \\\\ ∠D & ≅∠W\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have two more pairs of congruent sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= ZW \\\\\nBC &= YZ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to $Y$, and\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to $W$.\n\nFinally, we conclude that a correct congruence statement is\n\n$$\nABCD \\cong XYZW\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0921774a-ff9d-5760-93eb-13517043d509" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Unknown Quantities From Congruent Polygons"}] :question/id #uuid "f6e8ab4f-f21a-5c5e-9682-9f9b00e4fdc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-2911" :question/problem "Given that $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$, find $x+y$.\n\n![](images/f1/f133fecf2229bc78933ad0e73bb707dd17cad45ae087037b3a327e3ef4dbbc73.png)" :question/worked-solution "The statement $ABCDE \\cong PQRST$ means that $\\mathcal R$ and $\\mathcal Q$ are congruent. Moreover,\n\n- vertex $A$ corresponds to vertex $P$,\n- vertex $B$ corresponds to vertex $Q$,\n- vertex $C$ corresponds to vertex $R$,\n- vertex $D$ corresponds to vertex $S$,\n- vertex $E$ corresponds to vertex $T$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCD &= RS \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= m∠P \\\\\n82^{∘} &= (11y + 5)^{∘} \\\\\n77 &= 11y \\\\\n7 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nx+y = 2+7=9\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e2f2884-829b-5e09-8fc0-16556b177732" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Congruent Polygons on a Grid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdda2ae4-764d-52e7-9586-f498c1b6c1a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8b08ea40b8d23918440847ca2575c8f605d05c92b47972b06003f51caacf3da0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108148} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7b1d17-aec2-57c9-9aac-4ce9872dd490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4f/4f1d670db55d76164853ba9ed242adf1f66f9d6149733cd79a70eda463b6de40.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108149} {:answer/id #uuid "02d35653-e633-50c6-8e75-4ccc00005561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921